            <ArticleTitle>[Effect of endoscopic sinus surgery on asthmatic patients with chronic sinusitis and nasal polyps].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>139-42</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore the effect of endoscopic sinus surgery (ESS) in patients with chronic rhinosinusitis and asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Forty-two patients with asthma who underwent ESS and control group were investigated. Interleukin-4 (IL-4), interferon-gamma (IFN-gamma), soluble interleukin-2 receptor (sIL-2R) and soluble CD23 (sCD23) levels in culture supernatant of peripheral blood mononuclear cell (PBMC) were determined by enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of these 42 patients 32 (76%), their asthma was relieved greatly after ESS comparing with that before ESS. 14 of 21 patients (67%) taken regular medication for asthma before ESS reported that less medicine was used after operation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This study demonstrates that ESS has a favorable effect on asthma in patients with symptomatic chronic sinusitis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
